 { In this section we'll be covering a mix of situations where the minor pieces clash, showing the typical positional goals that you should either aim for or avoid. The first of these is trying to establish a superior minor piece. Here's a typical example where Black falls for White's plan. } 17. { -KEY- } Bg4 { White makes a probing move. In reality the position is equal, and Black can continue in a variety of different ways. } Bxg4 $2 { Playing right into White's hands. } ( 17... Ke7 $1 { This move, or a bunch of other moves, are fine. By keeping the tension between the light-squared bishops, Black ensures that White cannot establish a superior knight vs bishop dynamic. } 18. Rfd1 { Both sides keep the piece tension for now. } ( { If } 17... Bxg4 17. Bxe6 { then Black would play } fxe6 $11 { changing the structure and taking away the outpost on f5. } ) 
Rhd8 19. Kf1 Bc4 { Black might even give a check here just to show that their position is so solid that they can afford to zigzag back and forth with the bishop. } 20. Kg1 Be6 $11 { There is no way for White to make progress, since after } 21. Nf5 Bxf5 22. Bxf5 $11 { the opposite-colored bishops make it an equal and pretty dry endgame. } ) 
18. hxg4 $14 { Black's fractured kingside pawns leave two outposts for the White knight on f5 and h5. } O-O-O { Black plays a natural move, castling and taking control of the d-file. } 19. Rfd1 $1 { Moving the f1 rook is best, leaving the option for the king to step to the centre. } Rhg8 20. f3 Rd7 $2 { Black, unsure of what to do and in a very difficult position, starts to go wrong. } 21. Rxd7 Kxd7 22. Rd1+ Ke6 $2 { Centralizing the king seems natural, but in fact the king here is starved of squares. } 23. Nf5 $18 { White is winning. The Black king is completely paralysed, while neither their rook nor bishop have any targets to lock on to. The problems in this game stemmed from Black's inability to sense the dangerous dynamic that would unfold after ...Bxg4?. Instead, Black needed to keep the bishop tension in place. That way, the best White could have obtained was an opposite colored bishop endgame. } 
