 { The structure, with pawns on light squares, leaves Black with the much worse bishop. However, the presence of queens on the board confuses matters. Can you find the strong idea that White played? } 1. { -KEY- } Ne4 $1 { Capitalising on the key features of the position - the undefended Black queen, and the presence of powerful outposts on d6 and f6. } Qxd2 ( 1... Qd8 $2 { Black cannot afford to avoid the queen trade. } 2. Nf6 Kg7 3. Rxc8 Bxc8 4. g4 $16 { The outposted knight sets up a devastating kingside attack. With their last move, White stops ...Ng8 ideas since they could meet this with g4-g5, establishing a power outpost on f6. } ) 
2. Nxd2 { So far we've spoken about the superiority of White's bishop. However, White was also alert to the powerful knight manoeuvre that would be in play once the queens were traded. From b3, the knight will eye up the weak queenside dark squares. } a5 3. Nb3 a4 4. Nc5 ( 4. Nd4 { was also tempting, but after } Nf5 5. Bxf5 gxf5 $13 { the position turns out to be less clear, since Black has a couple of resources on the queenside like ...Rc4 using the power outpost, or breaking with ...b4. } ) 
Bc6 5. Na6 $1 { Given enough time, White can think about doubling rooks on the c-file. } ( 5. Rc3 $2 { White should not let their guard down, with this move throwing away all of their advantage. } d4 $1 { A clever temporary pawn sacrifice. } 6. exd4 Rfd8 $13 { Suddenly it's all to play for, with Black's pieces having sprung to life. Notice how the opening up of the h1-a8 diagonal is in Black's favour, while the newly created outpost on d5 may be very valuable in the near future. You may recall a similar pawn sacrifice in one of the examples in the 'Activitity & Passivity' section of this chapter. } ) 
Rfd8 ( 5... d4 6. e4 $16 { Here, since Black's ...d4 break did not simultaneously attack White's rook on c3, White can lock the centre down and cover the important d5 square. } ) 
6. Kf2 $16 { Bringing the king into the centre, and protecting e3. } Kg7 { Black lacks an active plan here, so they don't have much better than to sit and wait. } 7. g3 Kg8 8. Rc5 Bb7 9. Rxc8 Rxc8 10. Nb4 { The piece exchange has not eased the pressure. } Rc5 11. Na2 $1 { A clever maneuvering move, preparing to fight for the open file. } Bc6 12. Rc1 Rxc1 13. Nxc1 { White has obtained their ideal endgame, with both minor pieces superior to their counterparts. } Nc8 14. Ne2 Na7 15. Nd4 Bd7 { For now Black is holding the weakness, but the problem is that their king is too far away. } 16. Ke2 Kf8 17. Kd2 Ke7 18. Kc3 Kd8 19. Kb4 $18 { The b5 pawn cannot be saved, and once it falls the a4 pawn will soon follow. The key behind the victory was to use tactics to force a simplified middlegame, where both of White's minor pieces were superior to their counterparts. The finishing touch was activating the king to overwhelm the delicate Black pawn structure. Next up, let's take a look at a couple of examples of a knight dominating a bishop. } 
