 { This position was reached on move 16 in the game Mattison, H - Nimzowitsch,A (1929) . } 16... { -KEY- } Qxb5 $1 { Black doesn't mind undoubling the queenside pawns. He is focused on the weak c4 square and winning the minor piece battle. } 17. cxb5 Nc4 $1 { The weak square on c4 is such a strong outpost, since it cannot be challenged by an enemy piece. If you spot such a square, most of the time the best strategy will be to occupy it at the first opportunity. } 18. Bc1 a6 $1 { A quiet but brutal pawn break, fracturing the structure and carving out a semi-open file. } 19. bxa6 ( 19. Rb1 $2 axb5 20. Rxb5 Rxa2 $19 { Black is up a pawn and with a much better position to boot. } ) 
Rxa6 20. dxc5 bxc5 21. Ng2 { White drives the knight back into the game via g2 in order to play Ng2-e3 and try to dislodge Black's monster knight. } Nd5 $1 { White's position was already lost, but the arrival of the second knight provoked a quick collapse. } 22. Rd3 Rfa8 23. e4 Ne5 $19 { White resigned, since they cannot prevent large material losses. In this game we saw the link between color complexes, pawn structure, and superior minor pieces. Once Black made use of the structural weakness on c4, all his pieces flowed to strong squares, and he was able to quickly overwhelm his opponent. } 
