 { The technique used by Aron Nimzowitsch in the previous example has been well-known for many years. The following similar position was reached in the game Najdorf,M - Averbakh,Y (1953). Let's see how Black exploited his superior knight. } 22... { -KEY- } Bxb4 $1 { Seizing the opportunity to secure the superior minor piece. } 23. cxb4 ( 23. Rxb4 { was similarly problematic. The c3 pawn remains backward, while White's control of the semi open b-file is not relevant as the b6 pawn is well-defended. } ) 
Na3 $1 { Getting ready to invade along the c-file. } 24. Rb3 ( 24. Rb2 { This was probably the lesser evil, but here Black could quickly double up on the c-file. } Rc4 25. e3 Rac8 $19 { Sooner or later Black will play ...Rc1 or ...Rc2, when it's hard to believe that White will be able to keep both of their queenside pawns alive. } ) 
Nb5 { White is immediately overloaded with threats. } 25. e3 Rc2 26. a4 Nd6 $1 { The knight remains a monster and will invade at the right moment. } 27. a5 b5 $1 { Good technique. It's best to lock down the structure along the light squares to enhance the opponent's problem bishop. } 28. Rc3 { Trying to get some pieces off the board. White's only hope would be to trade off all the rooks and hope that the resulting position is too closed to allow Black to break through. } Rc8 $1 29. Rxc8+ Nxc8 30. f3 Ne7 31. Bf2 Kf7 $1 { Improving the final piece. Black follows the principle of 'do not rush', since White has no good active plan. } ( { But not } 31... Rb2 $2 { when after } 32. Rc1 { White suddenly gets activity and is right back in the game! } ) 
32. Rb1 Nf5 $1 { The knight begins its journey back to the c4 square to resume the torture. } 33. Kf1 Nd6 34. Rb3 Nc4 { From here, White's position quickly collapses. } 35. Kg2 f5 $1 { White's position is so bad that he is in fact in zugzwang! This is a type of piece paralysis, a topic discussed in the first section of the chapter. } 36. Rb1 { In the game White saw nothing better than to give up a pawn. } ( 36. Kg1 { loses to } Nd2 $19 ) 
 ( 36. Rd3 { loses the bishop after } Nb2 37. Ra3 Nd1 $19 ) 
Nxe3+ 37. Kg1 f4 $1 { Returning the pawn in order to shatter White's structure. } 38. gxf4 Nf5 39. Kf1 g6 40. Rb3 Ke7 41. Rb1 Kd7 $19 { Black marches their king to the d7 square to control the entry points along the c-file. Next he would likely have played ...Rc4 followed by ...Nxd4. White had seen enough however, and chose this moment to resign. I hope you too enjoyed following along Grandmaster Averbakh's masterful endgame play. By the way, it is no surprise to see Averbakh's precision in the later stages of the game. He authored one of the most highly acclaimed series of endgame books ever written, the 5-volume anthology 'Comprehensive Chess Endings'. It's an advanced read, but perhaps one to make note of should you ever wish to do a truly deep dive into this phase of the game. } 
