 { So far we've taken a look at situations where the knight has been the superior piece. However, in cases where there are pawns on both sides of the board it is usually the bishop that shines. The following position was arrived at in the game Spassky,B - Fischer,R (1966) . } 40. { -KEY- } h5 $1 { The winning move. Passed pawns on the flank are especially brutal for a knight to deal with. } Nh6 41. Kd3 Ke6 42. Ba8 Kd6 43. Kc4 { Black is left with limited options, as the White king is not only glued to the c5 pawn, but in fact it may advance further in the near future to target Black's a-pawn. } g4 44. a4 Ng8 45. a5 { Spassky recognises that his opponent has no active plan, so he improves his position to the maximum before searching for a way to break through. } Nh6 46. Be4 g3 47. Kb5 { Having improved his position as much as possible, the king now marches to the a7 pawn. } Ng8 48. Bb1 { A good picture of the problem of knight vs bishop. The knight can only play a defensive role, while the bishop can combine attack & defense. } Nh6 49. Ka6 Kc6 50. Ba2 $18 ( { Black resigned in view of } 50. Ba2  51. Kxa7 { . An endgame masterclass from Spassky, showing the flexiblity and scope of the bishop against a knight in situations with action across both flanks. Let's now take a look at another classic game, another battle between two former World Champions: Vasily Smyslov with the White pieces, against the 'Magician from Riga', Mikhail Tal. } ) 

