 { This position was reached in the game Smylsov,V - Tal,M (1964) . White is in serious trouble here. Black's central pawns are perfectly chained along dark squares, restricting White's knight while keeping full range of movement for his bishop. Still, the path to victory is narrow. Can you find the only winning move for Black? } 35... { -KEY- } a5 $1 { Correct! It's crucial to lock the structure to ensure that the pawn is both a fixed target and on a light square. } 36. Kc2 Be8 $1 { Black uses the tempo against the a4 pawn to improve his bishop. } 37. Kb3 Bc6 $1 { Black achieves the ideal configuration. The lone bishop has paralysed White's army. } 38. Ka3 Kf6 39. Kb3 Kg6 40. Ka3 { White has nothing better to do than shuffle his king around. } Kh5 41. h3 { White is now forced to change the structure on the kingside, in order to avoid a decisive entry of the enemy king. } Kg6 $1 { The singular job of the Black king had been to drive the pawn to h3. Having coerced White into this slight change, Mikhail Tal starts up on the next phase of his plan. } 42. Kb3 Kg7 43. Ka3 Kf6 44. Kb3 Be8 $1 { Here we go again! The bishop momentarily abandons the control of the f3 square, but all has been calculated. } 45. Ng2 ( 45. Nf3 { This natural try fails. Mikhail Tal had calculated everything to precision. } Bh5 46. Ne5 Bd1 $1 47. Ka3 Ke6 $1 { Optically it appears as if White has made progress, but it's a tale of deception. The White king cannot move without forfeiting the a4 pawn, while the White knight is limited to a single jump. } 48. Nc6 { Advancing pawns on the kingside with 48.g4 or 48.h4 would soften the barrier and eventually lead to the Black king penetrating along the kingside. } Bc2 49. Ne5 Kd6 { White's pieces are in zugzwang, so he must move one of the pawns. } 50. g4 fxg4 51. hxg4 Ke6 52. g5 Kf5 $19 { Finally White runs out of options. } ) 
Bh5 46. Kc2 { Stopping 46...Bd1+. } Be2 $1 { Now with the pawn on h3, Black has the nasty threat of ...Bf1 next. } 47. Ne1 Bf1 $1 { Now we see why earlier in the game Black had forced White to push his pawn from h2 to h3! } 48. h4 { Smyslov, a former World Champion himself, spotted that trying to save the pawn would fail and so took a different direction. Let's see what would have happened had he kept the pawn alive. } ( 48. Nf3 { In the game, Smyslov gave up the pawn and Tal went on to convert the full point twenty or so moves later. } ) 
Be2 $1 { A necessary move, escaping with the bishop before White can play Kd2. } 49. Kd2 Bh5 50. Kc2 Be8 51. Kb3 Bc6 $19 { We've come full circle. Black's bishop is back on c6, but this time with the White pawn on h4. I don't know about you, but these type of endgames are some of my favorite to study. Black patiently provoked Smyslov's pawn from h2 to h3, and then from h3 to h4. All to carve out a path for the king to enter and gobble up the defenseless pawns. In this game, we again saw how the bishop was able to use its long-range ability and dexterity of movement to overwhelm the knight, in a position where the action took place across both flanks. The next theme that we will touch upon in this section is the importance of color complexes in the battle between knights and bishops. } 
