Rxc6 2. Nd5 Qe8 { Black tries to ignore the knight. } ( { If Black plays } 2... Bxd5 { then White has the upper hand in the heavy piece middlegame after } 3. Rxd5 $1 Rd8 4. O-O { ± The problem that Black faces is that if they ever capture on d5, then White will be left with a strong protected passed pawn. } ) 
3. Qe3 $1 { A useful move before castling. Black is forced to react to the pressure against the e5 pawn. } f6 4. O-O Kh8 5. Rd2 { White has the better chances, with strong control over the open file and the better minor piece. This has been a straightforward line, but where it gets interesting is if we compare best play in this position with the next near-identical example. } 
