 { The position here is near-identical to the previous one. The only difference is that White's e-pawn sits on e3 and not on e2. The upshot of this is that the d4 square is protected, but the downside is that White's light square control is much poorer. } 1. { -KEY- } Bxc6 $6 ( { With the pawn on e3, giving up the light-squared bishop offers no advantage and contains a lot of practical risk. Instead, White would have got a safe and slightly better position with either } 1. Nd5 { or } 1. Na4 { . } ) 
Rxc6 2. Nd5 Qe8 { Black sidesteps the influence of the knight, and at the same time looks to target White's light-squared weaknesses on the kingside. Here White probably should play h2-h3, or if not then e3-e4 or f2-f3. Anything to increase control over the light squares. Let's see how quickly things can go wrong if White does not address the problems of the light squares. } 3. O-O $2 { Walking into the line of fire. The problem for White is that with the pawn sitting on e3, White has no protection of key light squares like f3. } Bh3 $1 4. Rfe1 Bg4 $1 5. Rd2 e4 $1 { Fixing White's pawns on dark squares, and securing an outpost on f3. } 6. a3 $2 { White, unable to find any way to break down Black's control of the light squares on the kingside, shifts the action to the queenside. However, this allows Black to launch a deadly attack. } ( 6. f4 { The seriousness of White's problems are illustrated by the fact that this is the top computer move. Something like this is very difficult to play as a human since after } exf3 { White is both down a pawn and has the worse structure. } ) 
Bf3 $1 7. axb4 Rh6 $1 8. bxc5 g5 $1 { Incredibly, Black has time to play this move. With it, they shut down both of White's major defensive ideas: Nd5-f4 and h2-h4. } 9. Qc3 f6 10. c6 { White might try to keep advancing the pawn, hoping to distract Black from the attack, but it's too late. } Rxh2 $1 11. Kxh2 Qh5+ 12. Kg1 Qh1# { The main takeaway of this position and the previous one is that when choosing between a knight and a bishop, it is extremely important to take a note of color complexes. In particular, you want to avoid a situation where your opponent is left with the bishop of the same color as where you are weakest. In this case, White was weak on the light squares and Black had the light-squared bishop. Such situations rarely end well. Let's round out the section with one last important technique related to color complexes. } 
