25. Nc5 $1 Ra8 26. Ke3 Ra7 { Passing time and hoping that White will not be able to break down this configuration. } 27. Kd2 Ra8 28. Kc3 { If allowed, the White king will march to a5. } a5 { Stopping further progress, but the a5 pawn is hard to defend in the long run. } 29. g3 Rg8 30. a4 $1 { Fixing Black's a-pawn and setting up Nb7 possibilities. } Rb8 31. Rd2 gxf4 32. gxf4 Rg8 33. b4 { White decides to bring home his advantage by creating a passed flank pawn. } axb4+ 34. Kxb4 Rb8+ 35. Kc3 Rb1 36. Nb3 Rf1 37. a5 Rxf4 38. Nd4 c5 $2 { The final mistake, allowing for a beautiful finish. } 39. Nxe6 fxe6 40. a6 Rg4 { It looks like I am going to be in time to stop the pawn... } 41. Rd8 $18 ( { Ouch! I resigned here, in view of } 41. Rd8 Kxd8 42. a7 { . There is no way to stop the pawn. We can see from this game that Black was facing an uphill battle once White was able to exchange the dark-squared bishops. From there, White kept increasing his advantage before switching to sharp tactical play to force promotion in the rook endgame. In this example we saw that White wasted no time eliminating Black's valuable bishop pair. In general, you wish to avoid losing this powerful duo without a good cause. In the rest of the section we'll cover a few typical situations to watch out for. } ) 

