 { A common mistake to avoid is giving up the bishop pair for too little material. } 1... { -KEY- } Bxd4 $2 { Losing the powerful dark-squared bishop for just a pawn spells disaster for Black. } ( { The right way to continue was } 1... Nc6 $1 { when after } 2. Be3 Nxd4 $1 3. Bxd4 Bxd4 4. Nxd4 Qxd4 $15 { Without either dark-squared bishop on the board, White lacks enough compensation for the central pawn. } ) 
2. Nxd4 Qxd4 3. Bh6 $1 Re8 4. Bc4 e6 5. Rad1 $18 { White has a powerful initiative, while the dark squares around the Black king are a game-ending problem. This example is a reminder of the 'color complexes' examples covered previously in this section. } 
