 ( { White can choose between eliminating the bishop pair with } 1. Nxe7 { , or retreating with } 1. Nc3 { . Which do you think is stronger? } ) 
14. { -KEY- } Nc3 $1 { At the chessboard every rule comes with many exceptions! In this case White should retreat since the knight on c3 is their only minor piece that is keeping an eye on the light squares, in particular the critical d5 square. } ( 14. Nxe7 $2 { Here White would be left with only the dark-squared bishop, a poor complement to their central pawns. As a result, Black can easily dominate the light squares. } Nxe7 $1 15. O-O Nd5 $11 { In spite of being a pawn down, black has no problems due to the dominance over the light squares. } ) 
Bd5 15. Be3 O-O 16. O-O $16 { Here Black's control over the light squares is much shakier. White's extra pawn and better structure leaves them with a near-winning position. This brings the section on minor piece battles to a close. The final two sections of the chapter will explore situations where aggression is called for, with the right path involving one or more positional sacrifices. } 
